<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Army of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1460s–<a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Black Army</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Army was the standing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, built up from the 1460s. Made up of Czech, German, Polish, Serbian and Hungarian soldiers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, light hussars, handgunners and Hussite-style wagon forts, it won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> for Matthias. Left unpaid after his death in 1490, it was disbanded in the early 1490s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-army-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Black Army of Hungary" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Black_Army_of_Hungary.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestor and core of the army appeared in the early 1440s, in the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>. Matthias is said to have taken the idea of a professional standing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army from boyhood reading about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. In his first years the army still rested on noble banners and the militia portalis, under which a noble raised one archer for every twenty serf-lots.</p><p>In 1458 Matthias borrowed 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> whose tactics the Black Army later adapted. Under the Peace Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a> of 1463 John Jiskra, who led deserters plundering the north, was promised a royal pardon and two castles. The first mass hiring of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> came during the Bohemian Wars of 1468–1478.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The soldiers were mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>, Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and, from 1480, Hungarians. The core began at 6,000–8,000 men, reached 15,000–20,000 in the 1480s, and a military parade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1485 showed 28,000, of whom 20,000 were horsemen. That was far larger than the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a>, the only other permanent professional army of the era. Every fourth soldier carried an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a>, an unusual ratio, and the infantry sheltered behind large pavises.</p><p>Matthias paid for it by raising the serfs' taxes and basing them on households instead of portae. His annual income reached 650,000 florins by one count, though historians have speculated that it could reach about 800,000 in a good year. He also kept a river fleet, which numbered 360 vessels in 1479.</p><p>Pay was irregular, and unpaid troops sometimes left or revolted. In 1481 a group of 300 horsemen joined the opposing Holy Roman forces. The army conquered large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1485, and more than half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Crown of Bohemia</a>, and it won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-breadfield">Battle of Breadfield</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1479.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Matthias died on 6 April 1490. His son John Corvinus commanded the Black Army, but the barons invited other claimants and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vladislaus II of Bohemia</a>, and the army's core was then stuck in Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. The captains Blaise Magyar and Paul Kinizsi joined rival claimants. The nobility in parliament cut taxes by 70 to 80 percent, so Vladislaus could not pay for the army, and its end followed Matthias's death. Left unpaid, soldiers pillaged Hungarian and later Austrian villages.</p><p>Several theories explain the name, one tracing it to a captain nicknamed Black John Haugwitz. After the dissolution the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> also dismantled the administration, garrisons went unpaid and fortresses fell into disrepair.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Army of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Matthias Corvinus created a standing mercenary force later known as the <b>Black Army</b>, with heavy cavalry, Hussite-style wagon forts and handgunners, which conquered Moravia, Silesia and Vienna, while his commanders defeated an Ottoman raid at the Battle of Breadfield in 1479.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
